---
import AfternoonLife from "../../components/graph/AfternoonLife.astro";
import PaperTrip from "../../components/graph/PaperTrip.astro";
import RequestLoop from "../../components/graph/RequestLoop.astro";
import { NOTEBOOK } from "../../i18n/ui";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="flows"
  eyebrow="Four diagrams"
  title="How it flows"
  description="Who is allowed to ask whom, what happens to an activity between being invented and being over, and what a sheet of paper does on its round trip."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Who is allowed to ask whom, what happens to an activity between being invented and being over,
    and what a sheet of paper does on its round trip.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Who asks whom</h2>

  <p>
    Every arrow that crosses into the house is the answer to a request the house made. There is no
    inbound path: the panel writes a row, and the hub finds it the next time it asks. That is what
    makes the dashboard inert — changing a setting stores a new state and makes nothing happen in a
    room.
  </p>

  <RequestLoop
    title="Who asks whom"
    desc="The house holds a hub, two e-paper displays, a button, a printer and a scanner. The hub sends one arrow out to the panel and API in Azure, and the panel answers inside the reply. Inside Azure the panel passes work to the model router, the router to Content Safety, and Content Safety to Microsoft Foundry. A parent in a browser writes a row to the panel. No arrow starts in Azure and ends in the house."
    caption="Both edges across the middle begin on the left. A parent's approval enters at the top right and stops there: it is a row, not an instruction."
    house="The house"
    cloud="Azure, EU region"
    parent={{ title: "A parent, in a browser", sub: ["approves, and sets the hours"] }}
    writes="writes a row"
    displays={{ title: "e-paper displays", sub: ["a finished 1-bit bitmap"] }}
    button={{ title: "a button", sub: ["answered in the reply"] }}
    hub={{
      title: "Hub",
      sub: ["clock · sealing keys · print queue", "the only thing that starts work"],
    }}
    paper={{ title: "printer · scanner", sub: ["one A4 sheet at a time"] }}
    shows="shows"
    press="a press"
    sheet="out, and back in"
    api={{ title: "panel + API", sub: ["holds decisions, starts nothing"] }}
    router={{ title: "model router", sub: ["the only module allowed a model SDK"] }}
    gate={{ title: "Content Safety", sub: ["mints the screened payload"] }}
    foundry={{ title: "Microsoft Foundry", sub: ["language · vision · image"] }}
    asks={["asks,", "on its own clock"]}
    answers={["answers,", "inside the reply"]}
  />

  <p class="note">
    No model runs on the device, and an import-level test refuses an on-device runtime in every
    package including the router. The trade is stated plainly: one inference path instead of two, no
    weights to ship — at the price that a page which comes back while the cloud is unreachable stays
    unread until it is reachable.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The life of an activity</h2>

  <p>
    One document, written in a single pass before anybody has read a word of it. It holds every
    moment, three lengths of each, a four-rung ladder of help under each, and a way out. Nothing is
    added while it runs — which is what lets the same moment be shortened, or put on a display that
    holds forty words, and still reach the same ending.
  </p>

  <AfternoonLife
    title="The life of an activity"
    desc="The house asks for an idea. A model writes the whole activity in one pass. Six checks and a second model reread it, and a refusal sends it back to be written again. It then waits for a parent, who approves. The house begins it. A moment runs, and what came back either starts the next moment, or is sent up as an ask which returns a continuation, or closes the activity on its object."
    caption="The two edges that turn back are where the cost is. A refusal on the left buys another whole devise; an ask on the right is the activity admitting it is not finished being written."
    idea={{ title: "The house asks for an idea", sub: ["it keeps a few ready"] }}
    devise={{
      title: "A model writes the whole activity",
      sub: ["every moment at once — 68–157 s"],
    }}
    checks={{
      title: "Six checks, then a second model",
      sub: ["rereads the whole thing — 14.4–22.8 s"],
    }}
    waits={{ title: "It waits for a parent", sub: ["nothing prints, nothing wakes"] }}
    begins={{ title: "The house begins it", sub: ["only if the whole length still fits"] }}
    moment={{ title: "A moment runs", sub: ["say · hand over · collect"] }}
    cameBack={{ title: "What came back", sub: ["marks, or blank"] }}
    ends={{ title: "It ends on the object", sub: ["the same ending, however it went"] }}
    ask={{
      title: "ask",
      sub: [
        "the house sends up the blank and the",
        "page that came back, and receives a",
        "continuation in the same format",
      ],
    }}
    refused="refused"
    passes="passes"
    approving="approving writes a row"
    nextMoment="the next moment"
    askEdge="ask"
    continuation="a continuation, in the same format"
    closes="it closes"
  />

  <p>
    Two edges are worth reading twice. A refusal costs a whole new devise, which is why a check that
    reads a phrasing instead of a fact is expensive: one such check was the most frequent refusal
    until it was fixed. And an activity begins only if its whole length still fits before the hour
    the parent set — thirty minutes before that hour the way out starts, whatever has been reached,
    and no display says it was shortened.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The paper round trip</h2>

  <p>
    The part with the least machinery in it. Nothing is printed on the paper for a machine to find,
    so there is nothing to detect, rectify or decode.
  </p>

  <PaperTrip
    title="The paper round trip"
    desc="Words that have already passed the safety gate go into one image call, which draws the whole sheet. The sheet is printed and its blank is kept. Somebody writes on it, or does not. The scanner takes it back, both images go to a model, and the answer is either marks or blank. That answer is what the next words are written from."
    caption="One box in the ring is not machinery, and nothing in the system can make it happen."
    words={{ title: "Words", sub: ["already through the safety gate"] }}
    drawn={{ title: "One image call", sub: ["the whole A4 sheet, in one pass", "19–33 s"] }}
    printed={{
      title: "Printed",
      sub: ["the blank is kept: it is what the", "reading is compared against"],
    }}
    person={{ title: "Somebody writes on it", sub: ["or does not"] }}
    scanned={{ title: "Scanner", sub: ["≈ 26 s"] }}
    read={{ title: "Two images to a model", sub: ["what is different? — 4.4–5.5 s"] }}
    outcome={{ title: "marks · blank", sub: ["the whole vocabulary,", "then the reading is gone"] }}
    feeds="and the next words are written from it"
  />

  <p>
    The reading is gone in the strong sense: not written to storage, not to a log, not anywhere a
    person could read it later. Not a count and not a fraction either, because a count of somebody's
    marks is one step from a score.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    Measured with a finger on a real button: press to reading on the display, about 35 s, of which
    26 s is the scanner. The floor is the scanner plus up to one wake cycle of the display, and the
    firmware adds 5–11 s to any refresh interval you ask for.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The calls to a model, in order</h2>

  <p>
    The three diagrams above say who talks to whom. This one says how many times and with what. One
    activity costs six calls to a model, and each carries a text written by hand that can be wrong
    on its own. When what arrives on the table is no good, the useful question is not which model
    answered: it is which of the six texts asked badly.
  </p>

  <figure class="graph">
    <div class="graph-frame" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-labelledby="calls-t">
      <img
        src="/images/calls-en.svg"
        alt="The panel asks the model which method to build out of, sending sixty names and no records, and gets back a form and a move. It then sends the whole activity, about 34 000 characters, and gets back one JSON document. If the format cannot read it, the answer goes back up as it was together with the refusal. If a check refuses it, the document goes back up with the complaints, once and no more. Finally the document's own words go to the safety gate, which lets them through or blocks them."
        width="833"
        height="721"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption id="calls-t">
      The two optional boxes hold four calls, and they are paid for only when something has refused
      the answer. The other two calls, and the gate, are paid for every time.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The two optional calls are the instructive ones. A refusal that comes back every activity is not
    a defect in the model: it is a sentence in the prompt asking for something impossible, or one
    that fails to say something necessary. A refusal of that kind held the research loop up for two
    days, and the missing rule was a single line — the order the moments are walked in.
  </p>

  <p>
    A notebook in the repository shows the text sent on every call and the answer that came back,
    whole: <a href={NOTEBOOK}>research/officina.ipynb</a>, which GitHub opens already run. It records
    the calls at the router rather than at each agent, so none escapes it, and it writes the whole
    conversation to a file. The last run's outputs are stored in the notebook, so it reads without
    running anything and without credentials.
  </p>
</Base>
